WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:07.330
Today I'll loke Kenosha, who is a psychiatrist
practicing in Boston and on the faculty at

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:12.570
Harvard Medical School. Also the cofounder
of healthy Gamer, an organization that uses

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:18.739
meditation and the latest in neuroscience
in psychotherapy advances to help video gamers

00:00:18.739 --> 00:00:24.020
take back control of their lives. Although
great to have you on. Thanks a lot David.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:29.080
So we have heard this week in the wake of
a couple of, of mass shootings, once again,

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:35.300
this idea of we should be looking at violence
in video games as a possible cause of the,

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.640
the real world violence that was committed
by these young individuals. So there's a few

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:44.030
different things that I've been talking about.
One is do we even know anything about the

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.990
gaming history of these individuals? If we
don't, then what are we even really talking

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:54.000
about? Number two, even if we did that would
not necessarily be a causal relationship.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.470
And there's quite a bit of scholarship actually
about the link between violence in video games

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:04.420
and other forms of media and real-world violence.
And then beyond that, when we look, particularly

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:11.540
at El Paso, you had really what were to me,
uh, white nationalist anti-immigrant motivations,

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:12.770
which don't know for sure,

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:18.260
but that I've not seen that as a theme of
any video games I've heard of. Nevertheless.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.600
So where can we, what's a good entry point
for us if we want to think about this from

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:28.430
a scientific and medical perspective to even
start considering whether this is a possibility?

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:33.229
Absolutely. So that's a fantastic question.
I think the best entry point is there was

00:01:33.229 --> 00:01:38.229
a study that was actually published this year,
which is something called a Meta analysis,

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:44.409
which pools data from lots of different studies
and sees if we can draw an overarching conclusion.

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:50.040
Um, and there was a study that was done this
year that basically showed that there if there

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.110
is an effect, which it's not really clear
whether there is or isn't, the effect is incredibly

00:01:55.110 --> 00:02:01.630
small. And specifically what video games do
is in a very small way increase internal feelings

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:06.750
of aggression. Right. Um, but the, the interesting
thing about the study is that the reason that

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:10.549
the effect size is so small is because there
have been many, many studies done over the

00:02:10.549 --> 00:02:14.269
last decade that have really conflicting results.

00:02:14.269 --> 00:02:18.969
So one study done in 2015 basically, which
was a very rigorous study found that there

00:02:18.969 --> 00:02:25.840
was no effect of increasing violent tendencies
or even feelings of aggression after playing

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.439
video games. There are some FMRs studies which
are sort of functional neuroscience imaging

00:02:31.439 --> 00:02:37.669
studies, which show that playing video games
actually suppress our emotional, uh, the emotional

00:02:37.669 --> 00:02:40.940
parts of our brain. And this is why like,
you know, if you've had a long day and you're

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:45.200
stressed out and you go home and you play
a video game, like that helps you feel better

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:50.480
because it actually shuts off emotional parts
of your brain. Uh, and so let me dig into

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.419
that a little because there's already a lot
there that I think is relevant to talk about.

00:02:53.419 --> 00:02:59.230
So you talked about the possibility of being
linked to aggression in some general sense

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:03.859
in some studies when I interviewed Eric Shara
from the University of Massachusetts six years

00:03:03.859 --> 00:03:09.680
ago whose perspective is more from a communication
sociology perspective, she at the time was

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:15.120
involved in a lot of similar scholarship,
which suggested at a very broad level that

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:22.409
there are some studies which show that video
games can sort of, um, uh, set off or encourage

00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:27.299
aggressive behavior in people with certain
predispositions already.

00:03:27.299 --> 00:03:32.720
Uh, but that anything beyond that does not
seem to be supported by the scholarship. It

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.900
sounds like you're not saying the same thing,
but you're sort of thinking along the same

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.919
lines in your interpretation. Well, yeah.
So I think the big difference is that over

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:45.260
the last six years we've had way more studies
and, and the simple fact is that they're highly

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:49.919
conflicting, which generally speaking suggests
that, you know, so you have some studies that

00:03:49.919 --> 00:03:54.449
show that there's no link. You have some studies
that show that there are increasing feelings

00:03:54.449 --> 00:04:00.370
of aggression, but feelings of aggression
