00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:06.099
So there's a big gap in, even if you say that
a video game increases feelings of aggression,

00:04:06.099 --> 00:04:10.669
which is easily disputed. Um, the effect is
relatively small and then it's a big

00:04:10.669 --> 00:04:17.110
jump to say feelings of aggression increased
or correlate to real world violence. Like

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:21.459
so you could look at even something like sporting
events, like people have high feelings of

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:29.880
aggression during sporting events, but we
don't blame like football for mass shootings.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.903
And so that causative link is still like it's,
it's a huge cost at a point yet. Although

00:04:33.903 --> 00:04:40.160
I wonder if we could blame some of those escalated
feelings of aggression during sporting events

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:46.060
for fights during and after the sporting events
themselves, which is quite a more common phenomenon.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:50.500
Absolutely right. But in that case, you have
a, you have a temporal link because you have

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:55.261
sports fans that are inebriated and, and really
gung ho about their, their team and then they

00:04:55.261 --> 00:05:01.480
get into fights. But that's, that's a far
cry from, from video games. Let me propose

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:06.870
something else. Not as something I think is
the case, but that I I think should be considered

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:12.980
if we're opening up the store, which is, is
it possible that playing a violent video game

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:17.120
could actually be a substitute for committing
real world violence?

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:22.930
And I'm asking this not in a rhetorical sense,
like is that a possibility? Yes, absolutely.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:29.010
So I think that there's actually neuroscience
data to suggest that that is the case. So

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:34.150
when someone feels aggressive, like I was
saying earlier, like when you feel like aggressive

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:39.200
or pissed off about something, playing a video
game, like why are they fun, why are they

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:44.700
recreational? It's because they sort of, they,
they decrease our negative experience of emotion

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:49.940
in the moment, which is exactly what recreation
is designed to do. So I've worked with a lot

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:54.510
of gamers and I've actually found the, so
I started out sort of working with people

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:58.070
around helping them with video game addiction.
But the more I've worked with gamers over

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:04.070
the last year, I've started to appreciate
more and more positive effects of video games.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:07.320
And I think that that's an area of research
which is like completely untouched because

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:08.910
everyone's focusing on addiction.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:12.870
Now. People are focusing on violence, but
there are a lot of potential positive effects

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:16.390
of video games, which we were just sort of
scratching the surface for you. I mean it

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:22.030
feels weird to me too to be coming out and,
and really trying to expose the, the sort

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:27.480
of lack of substance between behind starting
to blame video games as a sort of concerted

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:32.930
talking point for these shootings. And the
reason it feels weird is because as a student

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:38.530
of communication, uh, there are so many areas
